#### Interpretation Questions ----
"
1. 
Compare the 5-gene and 272-gene ASTRAL species trees. 
How do the node support values and quartet pie charts (Q1-3) change as the number of loci increases? 
Why are 5 loci insufficient for summary coalescent methods?

The 5-gene ASTRAL tree branches are primarily domianted by the main quartet topology, while the 272-gene ASTRAL tree has much more support at almost every branch for the q2 alternative quartet 
topology and even for the q3 topology. Even the very first group where the Brassavola genus branches off has almost a third of each quartet topology in the 272-gene 
ASTRAL tree comapred to the 5-gene astral tree. So, we generally see a confident tree with dominance of the main quartet topology become less confident by adding more loci. 
We generally see that the q2 and q3 topologies are have similar frequencies in the 272-gene tree, suggesting that there is not 'introgression' overall, but that there is also
not high confidence for most species except genera Calypso. 
The support values generally increase in the 272-gene astral tree and are less smoothed. Especially at the early nodes.
5-loci are insufficient for these summary coalescent methods because these taxa have a noisy unresolved taxonomy with varibility in loci and probably also linkage.
More generally, 5-loci are insufficient because it implies that there will smoothing of the taxonomy, creating estimation errors which over-support species assigments.
The 272-gene tree allows the tree to have higher resolution and be more predictive.

2. 
Examine a node in the 272-gene ASTRAL tree that exhibits significant discordance (where Q1 < 60%). Look at the relative proportions of Q2 and Q3. 
Are Q2 and Q3 approximately equal, or is one significantly larger than the other?
What biological process does this pattern suggest?

The node where Cremastra saprophytica and Cremastra aphylla diverge has less that 60% Q1 and about even proportions of Q2(~40%) and Q1 (~20%).
I think this represents the classic/neutral gene flow, with maybe slight introgression/systematic error given that Q2 appears to be greater than Q3.

3.
Identify any nodes where the 272-gene Maximum Likelihood supermatrix tree (IQ-TREE) conflicts with the 272-gene ASTRAL species tree. 
Does the supermatrix tree show 100% Ultrafast Bootstrap support for a node that exhibits high quartet discordance in ASTRAL? 
Explain why supermatrix analyses can yield high support for incorrect topologies in the presence of ILS.

Across the supermatrix and astral tree, we see that the node with Corralorhyza bulbosa has 34% support on the astral tree but 100% support on the supermatrix.
Focusing on Cremastra saprophytica and Cremastra aphylla again, we see 100% confidence on the ASTRAL tree and concatenated supermatrix, while the quartet topologies
for this node is more variable.
Supermatrix analyses may yield incorrect topologies if there is Incomplete Lineage Sorting (ILS) because it combines loci, if I understand it correctly, and smoothes out
their differences for incomplete data, which results in underestimating the variability in loci. 

4.
Can you identify a genus in the trees that appears to be paraphyletic? Which is it, and how is it discussed in the Bot J Linn Soc paper?

The Oreorchis genus appears paraphyletic to Corallorhiza. The paper discusses how discerning if Oreorchis and Corallorhiza are sister taxa or paraphyletic has \
been a challenge because of differing results between ITS 
ribosomal sequencing. Character traits such as leaflessness have also challenged taxonomy because of the convergence of traits such as leaflessness on different genera.
Geographic barriers, variable selection pressures, and speciaition may have created the wide diversity of the taxanomic tree with varying convergent traits and genetic similarities, while evidence of hybridization is mixed. 
